{"name":"Gold Torch","hex":"#bd955e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"gold","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A heavy yellow elemental metal of great value, with atomic number 79.","A coin or coinage made of this material, or supposedly so."]},{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["☉ (alchemy)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gold"},{"term":"torch","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A stick of wood or plant fibres twisted together, with one end soaked in a flammable substance such as resin or tallow and set on fire, which is held in the hand, put into a wall bracket, or stuck into the ground, and used chiefly as a light source.","A similarly shaped implement with a replaceable supply of flammable material; specifically, a pole with a lamp at one end."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To illuminate or provide (a place) with torches (noun etymology 1, noun sense 1).\n To intentionally destroy (something) by setting it on fire, especially when committing arson in furtherance of some other criminal act (e.g. insurance fraud or the destruction of evidence).","To illuminate or provide (a place) with torches (noun etymology 1, noun sense 1)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/torch"}],"entries":[{"title":"Torch","blocks":[{"text":"A torch is a stick with combustible material at one end which can be used as a light source or to set something on fire. Torches have been used throughout history and are still used in processions, symbolic and religious events, and in juggling and entertainment. In some countries, notably the United Kingdom and Australia, \"torch\" in modern usage is also the term for a battery-operated portable light."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Lewes_Bonfire%2C_discarded_torch.jpg/330px-Lewes_Bonfire%2C_discarded_torch.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Torch","wikidata":"Q327954"},{"title":"Gold","blocks":[{"text":"Gold is a chemical element; its chemical symbol is Au and atomic number 79. In its pure form, it is a bright-metallic-yellow, dense, soft, malleable, and ductile metal. Chemically, gold is a transition metal, a group 11 element, and one of the noble metals. It is one of the least reactive chemical elements, being the second lowest in the reactivity series, with only platinum ranked as less reactive. Gold is solid under standard conditions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/330px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold","wikidata":"Q897"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)","wikidata":"Q208045"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Firebreathingtorch.JPG/500px-Firebreathingtorch.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Firebreathingtorch.JPG/1280px-Firebreathingtorch.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Firebreathingtorch.JPG/500px-Firebreathingtorch.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An unlit torch as used for fire breathing","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFirebreathingtorch.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Main_Reading_Room._Detail_of_frieze_of_winged_half_figures_with_torch_of_learning._Library_of_Congress_Thomas_Jefferson_Build